<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastern Thrace</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> is the European part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a> river and the Bosporus, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a> assigned most of it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1920, but after Greek defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> the Armistice of Mudanya in 1922 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> of 1923 returned it to Turkey, and its Greek population left in the exchange of populations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eastern-thrace.jpg" alt="Eastern Thrace" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Infestor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EstThraceWithinThrace.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
